0 Fixture: two 2x2 round bricks stacked on a baseplate, as a tree trunk.
0 Expected: clean. Proves the round-brick top studs are seen.
0
0 A 2x2 round part does not use stud.dat. It uses stud15.dat, one of the
0 laterally-truncated studs drawn to fit inside a 40-diameter cylinder,
0 and none of that family was in ldrcheck's PRIMITIVES table. So a 2x2
0 round brick emitted its four underside anti-studs (annotated by hand in
0 connections.yml) but no top studs at all. The lower brick therefore
0 looked fine -- it grips the plate -- while the upper one reported
0 NO JOINT and the pair reported CONTACT WITHOUT JOINT, on a stack that
0 is obviously legal. Same class of miss as the axl3ho* family.
0
0 JOINS names the two bricks directly, so this fixture fails rather than
0 passes if that table entry is ever removed again.
0
0 Heights: the baseplate's top face is y=0, and a brick is 24 LDU with its
0 origin at its own top face, so the first brick sits at y=-24 and the
0 second at y=-48. Using the plate rule (-8) here instead put the brick
0 16 LDU into the baseplate.
0
0 !LDRCHECK GROUND 8
0 !LDRCHECK FRONT +Z
0 !LDRCHECK JOINS 6143.dat 6143.dat
1 15 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 91405.dat
0 STEP
1 288 0 -24 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 6143.dat
1 288 0 -48 0 1 0 0 0 1 0 0 0 1 6143.dat
0 STEP
